export default {"id":"launchkey-level-11","number":11,"title":"Keyboard Splitting, Layering & Multi Part Architecture","skillBand":"Textbook","duration":"From the course textbook","summary":"The 61-key keybed on the Launchkey MK4 61 can be routed as two completely independent musical zones: Part A and Part B. These zones transmit on discrete MIDI channels, allowing musicians to perform isolated multi-timbral instruments or stac","modules":[{"id":"launchkey-m-11-1-1","code":"11.1","title":"Keyboard Splitting, Layering & Multi-Part Architecture","blurb":"The 61-key keybed on the Launchkey MK4 61 can be routed as two completely independent musical zones: Part A and Part B. These zones transmit on discrete MIDI channels, allowing musicians to perform isolated multi-timbral instruments or stac","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"launchkey-l-o-32","title":"Section overview","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"Section 11.1 — Multi-Timbral Dual Zones & Keyboard Layering","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Module Overview","level":3},{"type":"prose","html":"The 61-key keybed on the Launchkey MK4 61 can be routed as two completely independent musical zones: <strong>Part A</strong> and <strong>Part B</strong>. These zones transmit on discrete MIDI channels, allowing musicians to perform isolated multi-timbral instruments or stack sounds simultaneously across the entire keyboard in <strong>Layer Mode</strong>."},{"type":"heading","text":"Learning Objectives","level":3},{"type":"list","items":["Formulate the two-part architectural model (Part A on Channel 1, Part B on Channel 2).","Route the keybed exclusively to Part A using <code>Shift + Fader Button 6</code>.","Route the keybed exclusively to Part B using <code>Shift + Fader Button 7</code>.","Engage simultaneous dual-channel transmission via <strong>Layer Mode</strong> (<code>Shift + Fader Button 9</code>).","Detail the channel allocation scheme utilized by official Novation DAW multi-zone templates."],"ordered":true}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"launchkey-l-11-1-1","title":"11.1.1: The Dual-Part Architecture","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"11.1.1: The Dual-Part Architecture","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The Launchkey MK4 61 contains two internal keyboard routing engines:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+-------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                  DUAL-PART CHANNEL &amp; ROUTING MATRIX                     |\n+---------------+-----------------+---------------+-----------------------+\n| Keyboard Part | Default Channel | Settings Name | Primary Application   |\n+---------------+-----------------+---------------+-----------------------+\n| **Part A**    | **Channel 1**   | \"Part A Ch\"   | Primary Lead / Bass   |\n| **Part B**    | **Channel 2**   | \"Part B Ch\"   | Secondary Pad / Synth |\n+---------------+-----------------+---------------+-----------------------+</pre>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"launchkey-l-11-1-2","title":"11.1.2: Hardware Part Selection Commands","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"11.1.2: Hardware Part Selection Commands","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Switching between keyboard parts is executed using the secondary functions of the physical Fader Buttons:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                    KEYBOARD ZONE SELECTION (SHIFT + FADER BUTTONS)            |\n+-------------------+---------------------+-------------------------------------+\n| Hardware Command  | Zone Mode Selected  | Operational Keybed Routing          |\n+-------------------+---------------------+-------------------------------------+\n| **[ Shift ] +**   | **Part A Only**     | Keys transmit strictly on           |\n| **[ Fader Btn 6]**| *(Factory Default)* | **MIDI Channel 1**                  |\n+-------------------+---------------------+-------------------------------------+\n| **[ Shift ] +**   | **Part B Only**     | Keys transmit strictly on           |\n| **[ Fader Btn 7]**|                     | **MIDI Channel 2**                  |\n+-------------------+---------------------+-------------------------------------+\n| **[ Shift ] +**   | **Layer Mode**      | Each single key depression          |\n| **[ Fader Btn 9]**|                     | transmits on **BOTH Channel 1 &amp; 2** |\n+-------------------+---------------------+-------------------------------------+</pre>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"launchkey-l-11-1-3","title":"11.1.3: Layer Mode Mechanics","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"11.1.3: Layer Mode Mechanics","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"In <strong>Layer Mode</strong> (<code>Shift + Fader Button 9</code>):"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Simultaneous MIDI Transmission</strong>: Striking any physical key generates two concurrent <code>Note On</code> packets (one on Channel 1 and one on Channel 2) with matching velocity.","<strong>Sonic Stacking</strong>: In a DAW, this allows playing a rich acoustic piano (Track 1 / Ch 1) layered seamlessly with an ambient string pad (Track 2 / Ch 2) across all 61 keys."],"ordered":true}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"launchkey-l-11-1-4","title":"11.1.4: Official Multi-Zone Studio Templates","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"11.1.4: Official Multi-Zone Studio Templates","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Novation provides official multi-zone project templates for Ableton Live, Logic Pro, and Steinberg Cubase:"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Standard Routing Scheme</strong>:","<em>Track 1 (Bass/Main Synth)</em>: Configured to listen to <code>Launchkey MK4 MIDI -&gt; Channel 1</code>.","<em>Track 2 (Pad/Lead Synth)</em>: Configured to listen to <code>Launchkey MK4 MIDI -&gt; Channel 2</code>.","Local <code>.als</code> session templates exist under the training knowledgebase library."],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Operational Rules Summary","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["<strong>mustCover</strong>: Part A (Ch 1) and Part B (Ch 2) defaults; selection commands (<code>Shift + Fader 6</code> for A, <code>Shift + Fader 7</code> for B, <code>Shift + Fader 9</code> for Layer); official template channel plans.","<strong>mustNotCover</strong>: Mini model zone methods (Mini lacks physical fader buttons and uses alternate combinations)."],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Practical Guided Task & Assessment Checkpoint","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Task</strong>: In your DAW, instantiate two MIDI tracks: Track 1 on Channel 1 and Track 2 on Channel 2. Engage <strong>Layer Mode</strong> (<code>Shift + Fader Button 9</code>) and verify that playing a single keybed note triggers audio simultaneously on both tracks."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Checkpoint Validation</strong>:"},{"type":"list","items":["MIDI monitor confirms simultaneous <code>0x90</code> (Ch 1) and <code>0x91</code> (Ch 2) note-on packets per key strike.","Both DAW tracks illuminate active meters."],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]},{"id":"launchkey-m-11-2-2","code":"11.2","title":"Keyboard Splitting, Layering & Multi-Part Architecture","blurb":"Split Mode divides the 61-key keybed into two distinct physical performance zones at a designated split key. The lower zone transmits on Part A (Channel 1), while the upper zone transmits on Part B (Channel 2). This module details mode acti","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"launchkey-l-o-33","title":"Section overview","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"Section 11.2 — Keyboard Split Point Configuration","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Module Overview","level":3},{"type":"prose","html":"Split Mode divides the 61-key keybed into two distinct physical performance zones at a designated split key. The lower zone transmits on Part A (Channel 1), while the upper zone transmits on Part B (Channel 2). This module details mode activation, split point boundary mechanics, the default C3 split point, interactive split assignment, and latch-assisted configuration."},{"type":"heading","text":"Learning Objectives","level":3},{"type":"list","items":["Engage Split Mode via the <code>Shift + Fader Button 8</code> hardware command.","Formulate the boundary rule assigning the split key to Part B.","State the factory default split point (<strong>C3</strong>).","Interactively program a custom split point using the hardware key-capture protocol.","Utilize Shift-latching to facilitate one-handed split point selection."],"ordered":true}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"launchkey-l-11-2-1","title":"11.2.1: Split Mode Activation & Boundary Rules","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"11.2.1: Split Mode Activation & Boundary Rules","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Activation</strong>: Hold <code>Shift</code> and press <strong>Fader Button 8</strong> (silkscreened <code>Split</code>).","<strong>OLED Feedback</strong>: Screen displays <code>Zone: Split (Point: C3)</code>.","<strong>Zonal Distribution Rule</strong>:","<strong>Part A (Lower Zone)</strong>: All physical keys strictly <strong>below</strong> the split point transmit on <strong>MIDI Channel 1</strong>.","<strong>Part B (Upper Zone)</strong>: The <strong>split key itself and all keys above it</strong> transmit on <strong>MIDI Channel 2</strong>."],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">                         61-KEY SPLIT MATRIX (DEFAULT AT C3)\n   C1                                  C3                                    C6\n   [ | | | | | | | | | | | | | | | | | | | | | | | | | | | | | | | | | | | | | ]\n   ◄────────── ZONE A (Ch 1) ─────────►◄──────────── ZONE B (Ch 2) ───────────►\n             (Left Hand Bass)                    (Right Hand Lead / Chords)</pre>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"launchkey-l-11-2-2","title":"11.2.2: Interactive Split Point Assignment Protocol","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"11.2.2: Interactive Split Point Assignment Protocol","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"To set a custom split point (e.g., shifting the split down to <code>G2</code>):"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">INTERACTIVE SPLIT POINT ASSIGNMENT:\n1. Press and hold [ Shift ].\n2. Press and hold [ Fader Button 8 ] (Split).\n3. While holding both buttons, press the physical key you wish to designate as the new split point (e.g., G2).\n4. Release all buttons.\n5. The keyboard remains in Split Mode with G2 established as the first note of Part B.</pre>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"launchkey-l-11-2-3","title":"11.2.3: Latch-Assisted Split Selection","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"11.2.3: Latch-Assisted Split Selection","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"If two hands are required to locate a distant key:"},{"type":"list","items":["Double-press <code>Shift</code> rapidly to <strong>latch the Shift modifier</strong> (Shift LED locks bright).","Press and hold <code>Fader Button 8</code> with one hand.","Strike the desired split key with the other hand.","Release <code>Fader Button 8</code> and press <code>Shift</code> once to unlatch."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Operational Rules Summary","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["<strong>mustCover</strong>: Mode entry (<code>Shift + Fader 8</code>); default split point C3; split key belongs to Part B; interactive key-capture assignment; Shift-latch assistance.","<strong>mustNotCover</strong>: Invention of multi-split (3+ zone) modes on the 61."],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Practical Guided Task & Assessment Checkpoint","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Task</strong>: Engage Split Mode. Set a new split point at <code>F2</code> using the interactive key-capture routine. Play <code>E2</code> (confirm Channel 1 output) and <code>F2</code> (confirm Channel 2 output). Then re-assign the split point back to default <code>C3</code>."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Checkpoint Validation</strong>:"},{"type":"list","items":["E2 outputs on Channel 1; F2 outputs on Channel 2.","OLED screen verifies <code>Split: F2</code>, followed by <code>Split: C3</code> upon restoration."],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]},{"id":"launchkey-m-11-3-3","code":"11.3","title":"Keyboard Splitting, Layering & Multi-Part Architecture","blurb":"Combining keyboard Split Mode with the internal Arpeggiator engine allows a single performer to execute an automated, rhythmically clocked arpeggiated bassline or sequence in one keyboard zone while simultaneously playing un-arpeggiated, ex","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"launchkey-l-o-34","title":"Section overview","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"Section 11.3 — Integrated Zoned Arpeggiation","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Module Overview","level":3},{"type":"prose","html":"Combining keyboard Split Mode with the internal Arpeggiator engine allows a single performer to execute an automated, rhythmically clocked arpeggiated bassline or sequence in one keyboard zone while simultaneously playing un-arpeggiated, expressive leads or chords in the other zone."},{"type":"heading","text":"Learning Objectives","level":3},{"type":"list","items":["Configure the <code>Arp Note Source</code> register to isolate the arpeggiator engine to a specific keyboard zone.","Structure a multi-timbral Ableton Live session matching the official <code>LK4-Parts-with-Arp</code> routing architecture.","Perform a simultaneous arpeggiated bass and manual keyboard lead."],"ordered":true}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"launchkey-l-11-3-1","title":"11.3.1: Zone-Isolating the Arpeggiator Engine","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"11.3.1: Zone-Isolating the Arpeggiator Engine","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"By default, the internal arpeggiator is hardwired to listen to <strong>Part A</strong>:"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Settings Verification</strong>: Open Settings -&gt; <code>Arp Note Source</code> -&gt; Confirm value is <strong>Part A</strong> (Default).","<strong>Zonal Consequence</strong>: When Split Mode is engaged, keys played below the split point (Part A) feed into the arpeggiator engine. Keys played on or above the split point (Part B) completely bypass the arpeggiator and transmit normal polyphonic keyboard notes."],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">                  SPLIT + ARPEGGIATOR PERFORMANCE TOPOLOGY\n+───────────────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────+\n| PART A: LOWER ZONE (Below C3)     | PART B: UPPER ZONE (C3 and Above) |\n| - Channel: MIDI Channel 1         | - Channel: MIDI Channel 2         |\n| - Engine: Feeds ARPEGGIATOR       | - Engine: DIRECT KEYBOARD PLAY    |\n| - Output: Clocked Arpeggiated Bass| - Output: Manual Expressive Solo  |\n+───────────────────────────────────┴───────────────────────────────────+</pre>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"launchkey-l-11-3-2","title":"11.3.2: Multi-Timbral DAW Routing Topology","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"11.3.2: Multi-Timbral DAW Routing Topology","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"To execute this in Ableton Live:"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Track 1 (Bass Synth)</strong>: Set MIDI From to <code>Launchkey MK4 MIDI</code>, Channel to <code>Channel 1</code>. Set Monitor to <code>In</code> or Arm Track.","<strong>Track 2 (Lead Synth)</strong>: Set MIDI From to <code>Launchkey MK4 MIDI</code>, Channel to <code>Channel 2</code>. Set Monitor to <code>In</code> or Arm Track.","<strong>Engage Hardware Features</strong>:","Engage <code>Split Mode</code> (<code>Shift + Fader Button 8</code>).","Engage <code>Arp</code> and latch with <code>Shift + Arp</code>.","<strong>Execution</strong>:","Depress a bass note in the left hand (e.g., C1). The arpeggiator latches and plays a rhythmic bass sequence on Track 1.","Solo freely with the right hand above C3. Individual notes trigger Track 2 with full dynamic velocity and zero arpeggiation."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Operational Rules Summary","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["<strong>mustCover</strong>: Arp Note Source parameter; Split + Arp performance architecture; dual-track DAW routing; official template alignment.","<strong>mustNotCover</strong>: Invention of dual independent arpeggiators (the hardware contains 1 global arp engine)."],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Practical Guided Task & Assessment Checkpoint","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Task</strong>: In your DAW, instantiate a Bass Synth on Channel 1 and a Lead Synth on Channel 2. Engage Split Mode (Split at C3) and engage Arp Latch. Latch a bass note below C3, and play a melody above C3."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Checkpoint Validation</strong>:"},{"type":"list","items":["Lower zone continuously arpeggiates the bass synth on Channel 1.","Upper zone plays manual single notes on the lead synth on Channel 2.","Zero crosstalk between channels."],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]}]};
